

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next fork — [1mwhich runtime gets the logger[0m, because this repo has two that don't share a module system.

  [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is a plain classic script ([38;5;153m<script src="app.js">[39m, no imports). [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m is CommonJS Node
  ([38;5;153mrequire('./utils')[39m). There's no bundler and no build step, so "one shared logger" isn't free — it means picking a
  module format that works in both.

  - [1mBrowser only[0m — buys you logging exactly where the login flow and the production issues are, in one file, with no
    module-system change. Costs nothing structurally. Rules out nothing: if [38;5;153msrc/[39m ever grows, the logger can be shared
    then, at the cost of the conversion below.
  - [1mShared module, both runtimes[0m — buys you one record shape and one implementation. Costs converting the project to
    ESM: [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m gets [38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m, [38;5;153mindex.html[39m gets [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m, and [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m/[38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m have
    to stop using [38;5;153mrequire[39m. That's a repo-wide change riding along with a logging request.
  - [1mTwo loggers, shared record shape[0m — buys you logging in both places with no conversion. Costs duplicated code that
    will drift.

  Cheap to change later: adding the second runtime. Expensive: the ESM conversion, since it touches every file and the
  HTML.

  [1mMy recommendation: browser only.[0m [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m is a hello-world that prints a greeting and exits — it isn't part of the
  webapp, and it has no production issues to debug. Logging it now buys nothing and would drag an ESM conversion along
  with it.
